#{[{:topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "![](images/92/925a47f324868fb022b51ff490ccecc557e989054253ad643542a478deb094d7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.7$, what is the value of $\\cos\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n![](images/f4/f438a90ea7afe511562058f49f13af29722aa137a8032a22ac16ca6566688cb7.png)\n\nWe're given that $x=-0.7$ at the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -0.7\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Point on the Unit Circle"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "What is $\\sin 335^\\circ$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\sin{335^\\circ}$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle $335^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/a7/a7456b0617c3c03ad55403e58904be26d826c90611c1a323553fa5c9f3149d66.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = 335^\\circ\n$$\n\nis in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 360^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 335^{∘} \\\\\n&= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin{335^\\circ}$, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin{25^\\circ}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin{335^\\circ}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin 335^\\circ = -\\sin{25^\\circ}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "What is $\\cos 340^\\circ$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\cos{340^\\circ}$ in terms of $\\cos\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle $340^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/c2/c263e4358de9cd29f1a97795e840d06cf4b198cf53c0630c2c12c85de7f00490.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = 340^\\circ\n$$\n\nis in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 360^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 340^{∘} \\\\\n&= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\cos{340^\\circ}$, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta_R = \\cos{20^\\circ}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\cos{340^\\circ}$ must be *positive* because the cosine ratio is *always* positive in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos 340^\\circ = \\cos{20^\\circ}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "What is $\\tan 162^\\circ$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\tan{162^\\circ}$ in terms of $\\tan\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle $162^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/57/578c1aa397751d9cc6497e35443ff7b3508f3126f18c6650a26fbd4e74bde58e.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = 162^\\circ\n$$\n\nis in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 180^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 162^{∘} \\\\\n&= 18^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\tan{162^\\circ}$, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta_R = \\tan{18^\\circ}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\tan{162^\\circ}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan 162^\\circ = -\\tan{18^\\circ}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :topic/math-academy-id 204 :topic/title "Extending the Trigonometric Ratios Using the Unit Circle"}]}
